WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.250
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4486.

2
00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:02.500
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.750
<v CHARLOTTE WILLCOX>Okay, so testing.

4
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:04.167
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Hey.

5
00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:06.667
<v WILLCOX>Can you both say your names?

6
00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:07.083
<v ARSENEAULT>Chelsea.

7
00:00:07.083 --> 00:00:07.500
<v CAROLYN RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Carolyn.

8
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:07.917
<v WILLCOX>Okay.

9
00:00:07.917 --> 00:00:08.750
Sounds good.

10
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:15.000
So, Chelsea, you can go ahead and do your introduction and we can start.

11
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:15.282
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

12
00:00:15.282 --> 00:00:15.565
Alrighty.

13
00:00:15.565 --> 00:00:16.977
Today is July fourteenth, 2015.

14
00:00:16.977 --> 00:00:21.214
I'm Chelsea Arseneault with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU Libraries,

15
00:00:21.214 --> 00:00:25.450
and I’m here today with Ms. Carolyn Rigmaiden-Frank to talk about your memories of Mossville,

16
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:31.099
Louisiana as part of the project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

17
00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:35.053
And we are at the Rigmaiden Center today, which is named after your father?

18
00:00:35.053 --> 00:00:35.336
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

19
00:00:35.336 --> 00:00:36.466
<v ARSENEAULT>Named after your father.

20
00:00:36.466 --> 00:00:37.595
The Jacob Rigmaiden center.

21
00:00:37.595 --> 00:00:43.527
And I just want to thank you for your time and for sitting down with us and sharing all your memories.

22
00:00:43.527 --> 00:00:50.870
We’re going to start with some background information to just kind of get a handle on who you are and then get deeper into the questions,

23
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.000
if that's okay.

24
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.019
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, okay.

25
00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:53.528
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

26
00:00:53.528 --> 00:00:57.093
So when and where were you born?

27
00:00:57.093 --> 00:01:07.278
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I was born at Saint Patrick’s Hospital, but I’ve lived here in Mossville . . . well I lived here twenty years,

28
00:01:07.278 --> 00:01:14.407
from the time I was born until I was twenty years old, I left.

29
00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:24.593
And it was always . . . I always thought that I would come back and live here, but just circumstances and everything.

30
00:01:24.593 --> 00:01:36.815
I was most of the time right there in Lake Charles [Louisiana], so I was still close and could still come back and visit.

31
00:01:36.815 --> 00:01:40.380
But Mossville is a place that . . . small.

32
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.963
I mean, growing up we knew, everybody knew everybody.

33
00:01:44.963 --> 00:01:51.074
We all played together, had fun and . . . A lot of things changed,

34
00:01:51.074 --> 00:01:58.204
even between my time of growing up and my children's time of growing up.

35
00:01:58.204 --> 00:02:04.824
The things that . . . The toys that we had, the things that we played.

36
00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:12.463
It was just different, and when I tell them that, "Oh, we didn't do this,

37
00:02:12.463 --> 00:02:19.083
we didn't have this," they just can’t see the things that we did.

38
00:02:19.083 --> 00:02:29.269
A lot of our . . . the games and things we did, it was . . . we made up things, and played and did.

39
00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:32.833
So it was a lot of things.

40
00:02:32.833 --> 00:02:37.417
I loved it here and so did my siblings.

41
00:02:37.417 --> 00:02:38.944
So, it’s just . . .

42
00:02:38.944 --> 00:02:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What were your parents’ names?

43
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.264
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Jacob Rigmaiden, and my mother came from Branch, Louisiana.

44
00:02:47.264 --> 00:02:48.433
Alice Lyons.

45
00:02:48.433 --> 00:02:49.603
<v ARSENEAULT>Alice Lyons?

46
00:02:49.603 --> 00:02:50.188
L-Y. . .

47
00:02:50.188 --> 00:02:50.773
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>L-Y-O-N-S.

48
00:02:50.773 --> 00:02:51.357
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

49
00:02:51.357 --> 00:02:52.527
Got you.

50
00:02:52.527 --> 00:02:54.282
And your grandparents?

51
00:02:54.282 --> 00:02:57.206
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Henry Rigmaiden and Ida Dunkins.

52
00:02:57.206 --> 00:03:04.224
She was from Merryville, Louisiana, and my grandfather was here from Mossville.

53
00:03:04.224 --> 00:03:17.090
When the Rigmaidens came here in the 1800s, and they. . . I think they came from England, is where it was original started,

54
00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:24.693
and so they. . . I don't know how they ended up here in Mossville,

55
00:03:24.693 --> 00:03:31.711
but they did and it just . . . a lot of us just stayed.

56
00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:37.560
<v ARSENEAULT>So both sides of the family grew up in Mossville?

57
00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:46.332
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, just my father's side and his father, and his father was here in Mossville.

58
00:03:46.332 --> 00:03:54.520
My grandmother was from Merryville, Louisiana, which is close to the Texas state line.

59
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.444
<v ARSENEAULT>So how did they meet?

60
00:03:57.444 --> 00:04:01.538
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I don't know, [Laughs] I don't know.

61
00:04:01.538 --> 00:04:13.235
It wasn't very much talked about at my house with past things, so it's a lot that I don't know.

62
00:04:13.235 --> 00:04:22.007
My older sisters and brothers probably did, but it's a lot that I didn't know.

63
00:04:22.007 --> 00:04:27.271
I know that my Uncle Josh was a minister,

64
00:04:27.271 --> 00:04:38.968
and he would. . . I think he had churches down in Branch and all this little . . . what we call down east,

65
00:04:38.968 --> 00:04:45.401
and it was this lady that he knew that wasn't married,

66
00:04:45.401 --> 00:04:55.343
and he had picked out somebody for her, and Daddy said that he wanted to marry her.

67
00:04:55.343 --> 00:04:58.267
He wanted to meet her.

68
00:04:58.267 --> 00:05:01.776
I mean, they had never met.

69
00:05:01.776 --> 00:05:12.303
So, Uncle Josh took him to Branch, and he saw Mama once, and the next time they married.

70
00:05:12.303 --> 00:05:14.643
<v ARSENEAULT>How old were they?

71
00:05:14.643 --> 00:05:18.736
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>She was twenty, and he was twenty-five.

72
00:05:18.736 --> 00:05:24.000
And they moved back here and lived here.

73
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:32.731
<v ARSENEAULT>I was going to ask you when your family moved here, but it sounds like . . .

74
00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:33.896
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[laughs] Yeah.

75
00:05:33.896 --> 00:05:42.045
Well, Dad had been here, but Mama came in 1930 when they got married.

76
00:05:42.045 --> 00:05:44.955
<v ARSENEAULT>Where were your grandparents employed?

77
00:05:44.955 --> 00:05:46.701
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I don't know.

78
00:05:46.701 --> 00:05:50.776
I don't really know what they did.

79
00:05:50.776 --> 00:05:58.925
But my father was . . . he worked at Firestone, the plant . . . Firestone tire and rubber.

80
00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:03.000
And my mother was a housewife.

81
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.157
<v ARSENEAULT>How long did he work at Firestone?

82
00:06:06.157 --> 00:06:07.510
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh, twenty-five years.

83
00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.314
He retired from there.

84
00:06:09.314 --> 00:06:12.471
<v ARSENEAULT>And what kind of plant was it?

85
00:06:12.471 --> 00:06:14.275
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Firestone tire and rubber.

86
00:06:14.275 --> 00:06:16.980
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, it was a rubber plant?

87
00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:17.431
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

88
00:06:17.431 --> 00:06:21.941
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever talk about work or anything like that?

89
00:06:21.941 --> 00:06:23.294
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Not too much.

90
00:06:23.294 --> 00:06:32.314
I remember one time when they had a strike, and they were wanting the people to stay and work, whatever.

91
00:06:32.314 --> 00:06:38.627
And it was only. . . I think it was about six of them that stayed,

92
00:06:38.627 --> 00:06:44.490
and they said that . . . I guess the management was saying that, "Oh, gee.

93
00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:52.157
These the ones that stay, the ones that don’t want to work, don't work, can't work, sick.”

94
00:06:52.157 --> 00:06:58.922
And so that was the ones that stayed in the plant, and everybody else left.

95
00:06:58.922 --> 00:07:01.627
And I know that my father,

96
00:07:01.627 --> 00:07:09.745
<c.p>too, was a minister, and he wanted to leave out of the plant to go to his churches.

97
00:07:09.745 --> 00:07:18.314
So one time when he was leaving out, the picketers shook the car and was really angry with them.

98
00:07:18.314 --> 00:07:21.471
But, you know, nothing happened to him.

99
00:07:21.471 --> 00:07:28.235
But that was one of the things that they always talked about was that strike.

100
00:07:28.235 --> 00:07:30.941
<v ARSENEAULT>Were they just striking for union?

101
00:07:30.941 --> 00:07:31.392
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

102
00:07:31.392 --> 00:07:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So where was your dad a minister?

103
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:40.444
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Mostly his churches was in Texas, but he was ordained over here.

104
00:07:40.444 --> 00:07:45.889
He was over here at the Christ Sanctified Holy Church Morning Star.

105
00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:51.333
Because we had just the three churches when I was growing up.

106
00:07:51.333 --> 00:08:01.769
We had the Mount Zion Baptist Church and Saint Paul Methodist Church, and then we had the Morning Star Christ Sanctified Holy Church

107
00:08:01.769 --> 00:08:06.759
<v ARSENEAULT>So what was it like being the child of a pastor?

108
00:08:06.759 --> 00:08:13.111
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh, [Laughs] that was . . . Well, when I was younger it just didn't bother me.

109
00:08:13.111 --> 00:08:19.917
The thing of it was, I guess, because my father became a minister in 1945,

110
00:08:19.917 --> 00:08:27.176
and that was when the sister before me was born, so he was always a minister.

111
00:08:27.176 --> 00:08:31.713
That's what we knew him as, whereas the others didn't.

112
00:08:31.713 --> 00:08:43.056
And they could talk about when he was drinking and doing all sorts of things, but we've never been able to see him like that.

113
00:08:43.056 --> 00:08:48.046
But the thing of it was, we had to go to

114
00:08:48.046 --> 00:08:49.861
<c.p>church all the time.

115
00:08:49.861 --> 00:08:53.944
And you had to be in church all day.

116
00:08:53.944 --> 00:08:56.213
It wasn't like other kids.

117
00:08:56.213 --> 00:09:01.657
They didn't have to go sometimes, but we had to be there.

118
00:09:01.657 --> 00:09:04.833
I don't know . . . it was just different.

119
00:09:04.833 --> 00:09:13.907
Especially when I got to be a teenager it was really hard because it was certain things I couldn't do,

120
00:09:13.907 --> 00:09:17.083
or they didn't want me to do.

121
00:09:17.083 --> 00:09:21.620
So it was a little hard, the older I got.

122
00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:30.241
But, you just learn to accept that and to know that this was . . . even though it was his calling,

123
00:09:30.241 --> 00:09:37.500
but yet and still the family still have to live the way that he live, mostly.

124
00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:48.843
Because we knowing that everybody's looking at him, and in turn of looking at him and what he's doing, then they looking at us, also.

125
00:09:48.843 --> 00:09:59.731
So it was hard when we were . . . when we got to be teenagers and things because it was things that you wanted to do,

126
00:09:59.731 --> 00:10:02.000
and sometimes you couldn't.

127
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:02.929
<v ARSENEAULT>Like what?

128
00:10:02.929 --> 00:10:05.716
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, like go to certain dances.

129
00:10:05.716 --> 00:10:13.611
They used to always have hay rides during the summer time, and all the kids would go.

130
00:10:13.611 --> 00:10:15.005
I couldn't go.

131
00:10:15.005 --> 00:10:16.863
When Halloween, was . . . Oh!

132
00:10:16.863 --> 00:10:20.114
I never went trick-or-treating, and that was . . .

133
00:10:20.114 --> 00:10:21.043
<v ARSENEAULT>Why not?

134
00:10:21.043 --> 00:10:24.294
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, because Dad didn’t believe in that.

135
00:10:24.294 --> 00:10:28.009
They didn’t believe in that holiday, that day.

136
00:10:28.009 --> 00:10:31.261
And so we never could go trick-or-treating.

137
00:10:31.261 --> 00:10:42.408
And that was the one thing that I wanted my children to experience, and because they always say, “Well, why you never did go?”

138
00:10:42.408 --> 00:10:44.730
I say, “Because we couldn’t!”

139
00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:50.768
And so I really felt like I missed something, and I probably didn’t,

140
00:10:50.768 --> 00:10:58.664
but even though like now my granddaughter, her father is a minister and so I tell him,

141
00:10:58.664 --> 00:11:05.166
<c.p>“Let her experience things, because if you don’t then you never know what happens.”

142
00:11:05.166 --> 00:11:15.848
So that was one of the things that I didn’t like, because all my friends, they would come back the next to school.

143
00:11:15.848 --> 00:11:21.422
And they’d have all their candy and talk about what they did.

144
00:11:21.422 --> 00:11:31.175
And it was just good to hear them say that, but then I didn’t like it because I hadn’t experienced it.

145
00:11:31.175 --> 00:11:35.820
I didn’t know how it felt to do those things.

146
00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you get to go to the canteens?

147
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.556
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, I did.

148
00:11:41.556 --> 00:11:47.782
I got to go there because it was a time limit and . . .

149
00:11:47.782 --> 00:11:49.857
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, like a curfew?

150
00:11:49.857 --> 00:11:55.045
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, and if it was on . . . And a lot times,

151
00:11:55.045 --> 00:12:04.902
I couldn't go when it was on Sunday if it was open and the kids would go on Sundays,

152
00:12:04.902 --> 00:12:11.647
and a lot of times I couldn't go because I was at church.

153
00:12:11.647 --> 00:12:16.316
So everything was limited to what we can do.

154
00:12:16.316 --> 00:12:20.985
<v ARSENEAULT>What does your dad think about the Paradise Club?

155
00:12:20.985 --> 00:12:29.805
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, you know, he used to be out there in the world before he became a minister,

156
00:12:29.805 --> 00:12:35.511
and so I would think some of the people, he knew.

157
00:12:35.511 --> 00:12:43.812
This was just things that they did, so he would still talk to them and everything.

158
00:12:43.812 --> 00:12:56.782
They were still friends, and so whether he would think anything about how they were living their life, he still knew that that was his

159
00:12:56.782 --> 00:12:57.820
<c.p>background, too.

160
00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:07.677
So he was able to talk to them and they just still was able to communicate and do things.

161
00:13:07.677 --> 00:13:12.346
This was just things that he didn't do anymore.

162
00:13:12.346 --> 00:13:19.090
He didn't down anybody for what they did, and he would help everybody.

163
00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:28.947
That was one of the things that I really liked about him and saw in him as to how,

164
00:13:28.947 --> 00:13:37.767
if anybody would come and need any type of assistance or anything, he was there to help.

165
00:13:37.767 --> 00:13:48.662
Even with the voting . . . he wasn't really into politics, but he would talk to the politicians and see who was best,

166
00:13:48.662 --> 00:13:58.000
that would best serve Mossville and the people, and he would get this out to the people.

167
00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:02.136
I can remember when they used to have the cars,

168
00:14:02.136 --> 00:14:10.408
and they would pay these people twenty-five dollars for driving people to the polls and everything that didn't have cars.

169
00:14:10.408 --> 00:14:19.508
And I always wanted to do that because I would see people . . . I mean, you might take one family there, you know.

170
00:14:19.508 --> 00:14:21.990
Hey, you would've made twenty-five dollars.

171
00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:25.712
But he never would let the girls do that.

172
00:14:25.712 --> 00:14:26.953
Not his daughters.

173
00:14:26.953 --> 00:14:30.675
We couldn't get into any of this or anything.

174
00:14:30.675 --> 00:14:34.398
Now my brother did, you know, but we didn't.

175
00:14:34.398 --> 00:14:42.257
And if somebody would come and he had ran out of the money for paying for people to drive,

176
00:14:42.257 --> 00:14:55.079
well then he would call somebody else and see if they could give them money so that he could make sure that everybody that needed to would be able to drive.

177
00:14:55.079 --> 00:14:58.387
So he did a lot for the community,

178
00:14:58.387 --> 00:15:06.660
and I really thanked him and I know that they appreciated him, because they put his name on this building.

179
00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:13.691
They wouldn't have done that other than that if he didn't work diligent and everything for them.

180
00:15:13.691 --> 00:15:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Had he always been involved in politics?

181
00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:20.635
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>As long as I can remember, yes.

182
00:15:20.635 --> 00:15:30.500
And it had gotten bad at one time that we had to have police to sit at the house.

183
00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:34.135
Somebody had threatened him and it was

184
00:15:34.135 --> 00:15:36.731
<c.p>for until after the election.

185
00:15:36.731 --> 00:15:41.404
They had police parked near the home to watch . . .

186
00:15:41.404 --> 00:15:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What election was it?

187
00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:44.468
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Pardon]?

188
00:15:44.468 --> 00:15:47.744
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember what election it was?

189
00:15:47.744 --> 00:15:51.020
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, I don't remember what it was.

190
00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:53.828
It probably was the sheriff [laughs].

191
00:15:53.828 --> 00:15:59.444
That's what I'm thinking, that it may have been the sheriff’s election.

192
00:15:59.444 --> 00:16:06.465
But that was the only time that I know of that it really got bad.

193
00:16:06.465 --> 00:16:12.081
That things had gotten that bad with an election . . . especially out here.

194
00:16:12.081 --> 00:16:15.825
But you know people, they want the vote.

195
00:16:15.825 --> 00:16:20.505
So, you know, they want to get in office so . . .

196
00:16:20.505 --> 00:16:23.781
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember what it was about?

197
00:16:23.781 --> 00:16:26.589
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, Daddy really didn't tell us.

198
00:16:26.589 --> 00:16:38.758
And really we didn't know, if we wouldn't have seen this man parked by the house all the time, wondering why this person parked out there.

199
00:16:38.758 --> 00:16:43.438
And then he said, "Well he's just watching the house.”

200
00:16:43.438 --> 00:16:47.650
And that's as far as he would tell us.

201
00:16:47.650 --> 00:16:50.458
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he ever threatened or anything?

202
00:16:50.458 --> 00:16:52.798
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, he was threatened then.

203
00:16:52.798 --> 00:16:55.606
That was why they were there.

204
00:16:55.606 --> 00:16:56.074
Yeah.

205
00:16:56.074 --> 00:16:59.818
<v ARSENEAULT>Guess when you put your neck out there . . .

206
00:16:59.818 --> 00:17:00.286
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

207
00:17:00.286 --> 00:17:10.114
Because, you know, and then he would have all of the people here would vote for whoever he would tell them,

208
00:17:10.114 --> 00:17:21.347
and because they trusted him and they believed and they knew that he was doing that for their benefit and so the other people,

209
00:17:21.347 --> 00:17:28.835
I guess they felt that that was a threat for them, for the people out here,

210
00:17:28.835 --> 00:17:35.855
even though where at that time it had gotten to be more people out here.

211
00:17:35.855 --> 00:17:41.471
We had got all these subdivision—Bel Air, Lincoln Heights, and all that.

212
00:17:41.471 --> 00:17:43.343
They all was here.

213
00:17:43.343 --> 00:17:48.492
So it got to be quite a bit of people here.

214
00:17:48.492 --> 00:17:55.512
Not like in the beginning when it was just . . . I'll just say us few [Laughs].

215
00:17:55.512 --> 00:17:59.724
But you know, it was so . . . [many more now].

216
00:17:59.724 --> 00:18:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did it start getting bigger?

217
00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:08.396
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Around the time when they built the new school in fifty . . . Let's see,

218
00:18:08.396 --> 00:18:13.377
they opened the school in [nineteen] fifty-four [Frank clarifies it was fifty-five].

219
00:18:13.377 --> 00:18:18.358
So it was sometimes around that time when people started moving in.

220
00:18:18.358 --> 00:18:23.340
Because our school was right here in this lot and I was . . .

221
00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The original school?

222
00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:25.419
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

223
00:18:25.419 --> 00:18:27.095
<v ARSENEAULT>Or the new school?

224
00:18:27.095 --> 00:18:27.933
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>The original.

225
00:18:27.933 --> 00:18:28.352
Yes.

226
00:18:28.352 --> 00:18:29.190
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

227
00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:31.704
What do you remember about that?

228
00:18:31.704 --> 00:18:39.246
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>That was . . . Well, I started in the first grade, and here we didn't have kindergarten or anything then.

229
00:18:39.246 --> 00:18:43.017
And we had the three little buildings, wooden buildings.

230
00:18:43.017 --> 00:18:48.883
One was real, real big, and that one they had a partition in between.

231
00:18:48.883 --> 00:18:53.492
It had the first and second grade part of the partition.

232
00:18:53.492 --> 00:18:57.263
The other part was the third and fourth grade.

233
00:18:57.263 --> 00:19:01.034
And then it had the little building next door.

234
00:19:01.034 --> 00:19:03.547
That was fifth and sixth grade.

235
00:19:03.547 --> 00:19:06.899
It went up to the sixth grade then.

236
00:19:06.899 --> 00:19:09.413
And then it had the cafeteria.

237
00:19:09.413 --> 00:19:14.022
So that's . . . And I had . . . They opened up the new school,

238
00:19:14.022 --> 00:19:21.564
where Mossville High, in 1954 [1955], and I had the same teacher first, second, and third grade [Laughs].

239
00:19:21.564 --> 00:19:25.754
I followed her, or she followed me . . . whatever it was.

240
00:19:25.754 --> 00:19:36.229
Because she taught the first and second grade, and then the third grade was what she taught, and so I had her all three years.

241
00:19:36.229 --> 00:19:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have any favorite teachers you remember?

242
00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:43.682
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, she was one of them.

243
00:19:43.682 --> 00:19:44.909
Ms. Billups[?].

244
00:19:44.909 --> 00:19:49.818
And we had. . . That was in high school.

245
00:19:49.818 --> 00:20:00.250
Well, in junior high, yeah, that was a little different because you had more than one teacher.

246
00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:07.000
And in high school, Mrs. Rogers was a good teacher.

247
00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:09.188
<v ARSENEAULT>Was she your English teacher?

248
00:20:09.188 --> 00:20:09.625
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

249
00:20:09.625 --> 00:20:11.812
I think y'all interviewed her?

250
00:20:11.812 --> 00:20:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She's on the list.

251
00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:15.456
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah okay, yeah.

252
00:20:15.456 --> 00:20:21.766
I know my nephew liked that . . . Well, she used to be Ms. Calvin.

253
00:20:21.766 --> 00:20:23.708
She's Ms. Guidry now.

254
00:20:23.708 --> 00:20:26.135
Well, he really liked her.

255
00:20:26.135 --> 00:20:30.018
And when he graduated . . . He went to kindergarten,

256
00:20:30.018 --> 00:20:38.754
and when he graduated kindergarten he thought he was through with school, and he said "Oh, I finished!"

257
00:20:38.754 --> 00:20:40.696
He said "I graduated!"

258
00:20:40.696 --> 00:20:46.035
And I said "No, you still got twelve years to go.”

259
00:20:46.035 --> 00:20:48.947
But we had really good teachers.

260
00:20:48.947 --> 00:20:53.316
Most of them, when we did the new school,

261
00:20:53.316 --> 00:21:02.538
most of the teachers came from different areas of Louisiana, and most of them that was their first job.

262
00:21:02.538 --> 00:21:09.819
The thing of it is that they were very concerned about the students, our education.

263
00:21:09.819 --> 00:21:14.187
They really wanted us to go far in life.

264
00:21:14.187 --> 00:21:17.585
They really made a point to teach.

265
00:21:17.585 --> 00:21:27.292
It’s not like it is today where the teachers, some of them [don’t care] . . . and you’ve got some great teachers.

266
00:21:27.292 --> 00:21:32.146
But then you have some that is lax, you know.

267
00:21:32.146 --> 00:21:35.544
They could care less about the children.

268
00:21:35.544 --> 00:21:41.368
But all of ours were . . . I mean, they cared about the children.

269
00:21:41.368 --> 00:21:45.737
And that was the one thing, as getting older,

270
00:21:45.737 --> 00:21:56.901
that I realize and that I really appreciate all of them for all the time and the concerns that they took with us,

271
00:21:56.901 --> 00:22:02.240
so it was. . . We had a very, very good educational setup.

272
00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:15.345
I mean, even though I know the kids in Lake Charles just always say we was from the country. . . But we could stand with them with anything,

273
00:22:15.345 --> 00:22:16.801
you know [Laughs].

274
00:22:16.801 --> 00:22:24.082
A lot of times they would come and . . . well, they wouldn’t come but the teachers,

275
00:22:24.082 --> 00:22:30.392
some of our teachers were in organizations, the Delta’s and all this different

276
00:22:30.392 --> 00:22:38.158
<c.p>stuff so we were all included, and we would have to go to [Boston] . . . you know,

277
00:22:38.158 --> 00:22:44.468
we would have cotillions and all this different things, so it was good.

278
00:22:44.468 --> 00:22:47.380
High school was good for me.

279
00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:49.322
I really enjoyed it.

280
00:22:49.322 --> 00:22:52.234
I was on the honor roll.

281
00:22:52.234 --> 00:22:53.205
Honor student.

282
00:22:53.205 --> 00:22:56.117
And I was a majorette, and . . .

283
00:22:56.117 --> 00:22:57.573
<v ARSENEAULT>What is that?

284
00:22:57.573 --> 00:22:58.058
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Pardon]?

285
00:22:58.058 --> 00:23:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s a majorette?

286
00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:07.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, [Laughs] it's the ones that dance at the . . . at the games and everything [at half time].

287
00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:10.294
I don't know what they call them now.

288
00:23:10.294 --> 00:23:11.529
<v ARSENEAULT>Not a cheerleader?

289
00:23:11.529 --> 00:23:13.588
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, no. It wasn't cheerleader.

290
00:23:13.588 --> 00:23:16.059
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a spirit leader or something?

291
00:23:16.059 --> 00:23:18.529
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Maybe so. [laughs] Yeah, but . . . Yeah,

292
00:23:18.529 --> 00:23:31.294
they don't really have majorettes like they used . . . They still have . . . I guess they call them like a dance line or something that's there with the band and everything, but, yeah.

293
00:23:31.294 --> 00:23:36.647
And so high school . . . We just had our fiftieth class reunion in May.

294
00:23:36.647 --> 00:23:42.000
So we had it here, and it was good seeing people because a

295
00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:49.824
<c.p>lot of them I hadn't seen in years, so it was good to see them and talk to [them] . . .

296
00:23:49.824 --> 00:23:51.471
<v ARSENEAULT>What was that like?

297
00:23:51.471 --> 00:23:52.706
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>It was fun.

298
00:23:52.706 --> 00:23:57.647
At first I said, "I don't know if I'm going to that."

299
00:23:57.647 --> 00:24:01.353
But then I said "Yeah, I'm going to go."

300
00:24:01.353 --> 00:24:03.412
So it was just good.

301
00:24:03.412 --> 00:24:07.118
It was just really good to see the people.

302
00:24:07.118 --> 00:24:16.176
And the ones that weren't here I really missed because some of them I really wanted to see, and they weren't here,

303
00:24:16.176 --> 00:24:24.412
but it was good with the ones talking and talking about your family and showing pictures and all of that.

304
00:24:24.412 --> 00:24:31.000
It was just . . . It was a good . . . I guess [I had] a real nice time.

305
00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:38.181
<v ARSENEAULT>We've been trying to figure out like the geographic boundaries of Mossville.

306
00:24:38.181 --> 00:24:42.969
It seems like everyone has a different idea.

307
00:24:42.969 --> 00:24:48.354
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Oh . . . I guess to me . . . Let me see.

308
00:24:48.354 --> 00:24:57.929
For the east, we go . . . I guess where . . . I guess the tracks where the plant is.

309
00:24:57.929 --> 00:24:59.724
Where Conoco was.

310
00:24:59.724 --> 00:25:05.110
I don't know . . . What’s the name of it now?

311
00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:06.906
The Conoco VCM?

312
00:25:06.906 --> 00:25:08.102
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, Sasol?

313
00:25:08.102 --> 00:25:13.488
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I guess that was about where it ended there.

314
00:25:13.488 --> 00:25:24.858
That street that divides off the houses . . . Well, Bel Air was Bel Air then, and then the plant started.

315
00:25:24.858 --> 00:25:30.244
That's what I think it is from the east.

316
00:25:30.244 --> 00:25:42.213
And to the west, I think to me it went all the way to . . . well, I guess right now where . . .

317
00:25:42.213 --> 00:25:44.008
<v ARSENEAULT>Coach Williams Road?

318
00:25:44.008 --> 00:25:47.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>LaSalle Williams Road, yes.

319
00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:50.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's what we were thinking, too.

320
00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:50.500
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

321
00:25:50.500 --> 00:25:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was Mossville ever incorporated?

322
00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:54.266
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I don't know.

323
00:25:54.266 --> 00:25:56.376
Well, I guess it was.

324
00:25:56.376 --> 00:26:01.861
We had a post office, so I guess it was at one time.

325
00:26:01.861 --> 00:26:08.191
I'm not quite sure the post office burned or if it was in a storm.

326
00:26:08.191 --> 00:26:13.676
And once it . . . Once they lost it, they just never did rebuild it.

327
00:26:13.676 --> 00:26:15.786
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was the post office?

328
00:26:15.786 --> 00:26:17.052
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I don't know.

329
00:26:17.052 --> 00:26:20.006
That was way before my time [Laughs].

330
00:26:20.006 --> 00:26:20.428
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh

331
00:26:20.428 --> 00:26:27.601
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I mean, I heard them talk about it, but I never did know exactly where it was.

332
00:26:27.601 --> 00:26:30.133
<v ARSENEAULT>What would they say about it?

333
00:26:30.133 --> 00:26:34.775
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>They would just tell us that they had a post office.

334
00:26:34.775 --> 00:26:40.260
They were trying to tell us the things that we did have here.

335
00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:47.855
You know, we had a couple of grocery stores and other than that we didn't have very much

336
00:26:47.855 --> 00:26:49.965
<v ARSENEAULT>What were the grocery stores?

337
00:26:49.965 --> 00:26:51.653
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Uncle Josh had one.

338
00:26:51.653 --> 00:26:57.139
It was down at the corner where Prater Road meets Old Spanish Trail.

339
00:26:57.139 --> 00:27:02.624
And then Uncle Ira Garrett had one right here at the railroad tracks.

340
00:27:02.624 --> 00:27:03.890
Right over there.

341
00:27:03.890 --> 00:27:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What would they sell?

342
00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.448
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>They would sell everything.

343
00:27:08.448 --> 00:27:09.672
Well, grocery-wise.

344
00:27:09.672 --> 00:27:23.134
They would sell everything and well . . . a lot of times, we would go to and do our shopping over in Westlake [Louisiana],

345
00:27:23.134 --> 00:27:35.373
where they had Broussard's, which was at one time Piggly Wiggly, and that's where we would get our main groceries.

346
00:27:35.373 --> 00:27:42.716
And then we would go to the others if we needed something.

347
00:27:42.716 --> 00:27:46.388
Bread, milk, whatever, during the week.

348
00:27:46.388 --> 00:27:52.507
But used to go once a week and get groceries.

349
00:27:52.507 --> 00:27:54.955
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you grow anything?

350
00:27:54.955 --> 00:27:56.791
Does your family?

351
00:27:56.791 --> 00:28:06.582
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>My mother had a garden because she liked okra, and she would grow okra and peas.

352
00:28:06.582 --> 00:28:12.701
Things that she liked is what she would mainly grow.

353
00:28:12.701 --> 00:28:19.433
We did have . . . It wasn't a real big garden or anything.

354
00:28:19.433 --> 00:28:23.716
And before my time, they had animals.

355
00:28:23.716 --> 00:28:28.000
They would kill hogs and [chickens] . . .

356
00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:29.446
<v ARSENEAULT>Your mom and dad?

357
00:28:29.446 --> 00:28:29.808
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

358
00:28:29.808 --> 00:28:31.254
And they had chickens.

359
00:28:31.254 --> 00:28:36.679
Oh, I remember the chickens, but . . . [Laughs] They still had chickens when I came along,

360
00:28:36.679 --> 00:28:41.018
but I think they had gotten rid of all the other stuff.

361
00:28:41.018 --> 00:28:46.442
I don't remember any of them, but they talked about it that they had that.

362
00:28:46.442 --> 00:28:48.973
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the chickens?

363
00:28:48.973 --> 00:28:51.143
You laughed when you said it.

364
00:28:51.143 --> 00:28:54.397
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh, because we had to go feed the chickens.

365
00:28:54.397 --> 00:28:56.929
And they had a coop, a building.

366
00:28:56.929 --> 00:28:59.460
The little . . . that they called a coop.

367
00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:04.161
And you had to make sure that they were in there at night.

368
00:29:04.161 --> 00:29:09.223
Sometimes it was hard gathering them up to make sure they were in there.

369
00:29:09.223 --> 00:29:14.647
And then the feeding, they were all over you, trying to get to the food.

370
00:29:14.647 --> 00:29:18.625
You had little trays for them where you put their food.

371
00:29:18.625 --> 00:29:22.964
And there were times when they would kill a chicken and everything.

372
00:29:22.964 --> 00:29:29.473
I remember one time when my mother had died and Daddy wanted to teach me to do that.

373
00:29:29.473 --> 00:29:35.621
I said "Oh I can't do that, I can't kill a chicken!" [Laughs] So he killed it,

374
00:29:35.621 --> 00:29:39.960
then he had me to clean it and scald it with water,

375
00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:46.830
and you had to pick the feathers off, so. . . [Laughs] Oh, that was my first experience with doing that.

376
00:29:46.830 --> 00:29:47.915
And my last.

377
00:29:47.915 --> 00:29:49.000
Oh well.

378
00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:52.132
<v ARSENEAULT>He didn't make you keep doing it?

379
00:29:52.132 --> 00:30:01.526
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, no, no. I guess I wasn't too good at it, so . . . [Laughs] But those were just things that we did.

380
00:30:01.526 --> 00:30:05.105
I remember when we were little, we had.

381
00:30:05.105 --> 00:30:07.342
Well, we weren't little, little.

382
00:30:07.342 --> 00:30:17.184
But we were coming up and we used to have a rope tied in a tree at my cousin's house next door.

383
00:30:17.184 --> 00:30:24.342
We would all go over there, and we would run and catch the rope and swing.

384
00:30:24.342 --> 00:30:32.395
And one time my sister, she missed the rope and fell on her arm and broke her arm,

385
00:30:32.395 --> 00:30:37.316
and so then we all . . . It was a group of us.

386
00:30:37.316 --> 00:30:42.684
I say about ten to fifteen of us that would do that.

387
00:30:42.684 --> 00:30:45.368
We would do that every day.

388
00:30:45.368 --> 00:30:53.868
So when she broke her arm, well we didn’t know what it was, but her arm was dented in.

389
00:30:53.868 --> 00:31:02.368
So we all was just trailing behind her, going to the house to tell them that she was hurt.

390
00:31:02.368 --> 00:31:11.316
And I remember they took her to the hospital and she came back, she had the cast on her arm.

391
00:31:11.316 --> 00:31:22.500
And the thing that I remember mostly about that was because all the parents was there with the children waiting to see what had happened,

392
00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:24.737
what was wrong, and everything.

393
00:31:24.737 --> 00:31:31.447
I think about that because I think about how these days, people sue for everything.

394
00:31:31.447 --> 00:31:34.132
And it wasn't none of that.

395
00:31:34.132 --> 00:31:35.474
Nothing like that.

396
00:31:35.474 --> 00:31:43.079
And the rope came down though, but it was just still how good everybody was so concerned.

397
00:31:43.079 --> 00:31:48.000
But that was because of the closeness of the people.

398
00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:57.303
It brings to my memory these days when people tell me it takes a community to raise a child.

399
00:31:57.303 --> 00:32:00.241
And that's what that was like.

400
00:32:00.241 --> 00:32:03.669
Anybody in the community could chastise you.

401
00:32:03.669 --> 00:32:08.566
They didn't have to call your parents and tell you.

402
00:32:08.566 --> 00:32:17.379
They would chastise you at the point, and then you go home and you would get another scolding.

403
00:32:17.379 --> 00:32:34.028
But it was just . . . We were just such a close-knit community that really it just seemed like we didn't like it when all these other people start moving in because you didn't know anybody.

404
00:32:34.028 --> 00:32:40.393
Because before this, I mean, you could leave your doors open at night.

405
00:32:40.393 --> 00:32:46.269
You just wasn't concerned because you knew nothing was going to happen.

406
00:32:46.269 --> 00:32:53.124
<c.p>But then after people started moving in, then you had to start being careful.

407
00:32:53.124 --> 00:32:56.062
And it really changed the way. . .

408
00:32:56.062 --> 00:32:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that in the fifties?

409
00:32:59.000 --> 00:32:59.542
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Huh?

410
00:32:59.542 --> 00:33:00.626
Oh, yeah.

411
00:33:00.626 --> 00:33:01.168
Yeah.

412
00:33:01.168 --> 00:33:03.335
That's the forties, fifties.

413
00:33:03.335 --> 00:33:04.961
Well, thirties really.

414
00:33:04.961 --> 00:33:05.503
Yeah.

415
00:33:05.503 --> 00:33:09.297
So it was . . . Things change, you know.

416
00:33:09.297 --> 00:33:11.465
People want to advance.

417
00:33:11.465 --> 00:33:17.968
They want new things to happen so Mossville wouldn’t get left behind.

418
00:33:17.968 --> 00:33:19.594
That's what happened.

419
00:33:19.594 --> 00:33:23.929
And it just . . . We just had to adjust.

420
00:33:23.929 --> 00:33:29.890
Because when the new school . . . When Mossville High School was built,

421
00:33:29.890 --> 00:33:38.019
the high school children from Sulphur [Louisiana], Vinton [Louisiana], and Westlake [Louisiana] would come here.

422
00:33:38.019 --> 00:33:46.148
So we had to deal with knowing everybody at school to not knowing everybody anymore,

423
00:33:46.148 --> 00:33:57.529
and then we had to deal with high school kids and everything, even though we were going . . . We started as elementary.

424
00:33:57.529 --> 00:34:06.200
So it was just a different atmosphere for us, but I feel that we adjusted well,

425
00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:11.619
and we did the things the others schools did, and . . .

426
00:34:11.619 --> 00:34:18.123
<v ARSENEAULT>Why did the kids from Vinton and Sulphur have to come here?

427
00:34:18.123 --> 00:34:21.374
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>They didn’t have a high school.

428
00:34:21.374 --> 00:34:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why not?

429
00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:26.832
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>That was just the way it was then.

430
00:34:26.832 --> 00:34:27.311
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

431
00:34:27.311 --> 00:34:34.017
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Agrees] You see, once the children from here . . . Before we had the high school,

432
00:34:34.017 --> 00:34:40.723
they went to . . . I think they went to Westlake for a couple of years,

433
00:34:40.723 --> 00:34:48.866
and then if they wanted to go to high school, they had to go to Lake Charles.

434
00:34:48.866 --> 00:34:51.261
Well, I'm saying Lake Charles.

435
00:34:51.261 --> 00:34:51.739
Boston.

436
00:34:51.739 --> 00:34:56.050
But it was Boston High School at that time.

437
00:34:56.050 --> 00:35:05.630
And I think a lot of them went to Second Ward Colored School in order to get a higher education.

438
00:35:05.630 --> 00:35:11.378
And I know that when my sister and brothers went to Boston,

439
00:35:11.378 --> 00:35:21.916
they had to give my cousin’s address because you had to live in the city in order to go to that school.

440
00:35:21.916 --> 00:35:24.790
So it was sort of hard.

441
00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:34.370
And they had to get transportation for your children to go over there and so it was sort of hard.

442
00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:40.118
And then the parents worked hard to get us a school here.

443
00:35:40.118 --> 00:35:47.782
And so it was because of their work and talking with the school board and everything.

444
00:35:47.782 --> 00:35:51.134
That's how the school became . . . Got here.

445
00:35:51.134 --> 00:35:54.966
Because I guess it was like a center.

446
00:35:54.966 --> 00:36:04.067
From Westlake and then Sulphur and Vinton, and even the children from Houston River, way back behind Evergreen Drive.

447
00:36:04.067 --> 00:36:05.504
Way back there.

448
00:36:05.504 --> 00:36:07.899
All them came here, too.

449
00:36:07.899 --> 00:36:17.000
So it was, I guess, like more of a geographic location as to why they built it here.

450
00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:20.647
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about integration?

451
00:36:20.647 --> 00:36:30.373
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I can remember going to. . . [Laughs] I don't know why I want to say Walgreen's . . . Woolworth's,

452
00:36:30.373 --> 00:36:35.843
and not being able to eat at the counter.

453
00:36:35.843 --> 00:36:46.784
But of course, by us living over here, we never did really go to Lake Charles that much.

454
00:36:46.784 --> 00:36:53.471
We only really went to . . . if we needed to buy something.

455
00:36:53.471 --> 00:36:58.333
Other than that, we did everything over here.

456
00:36:58.333 --> 00:37:01.373
Westlake had stores, clothing stores.

457
00:37:01.373 --> 00:37:12.314
If you didn't want to go all the way to Lake Charles and . . . So a lot of things . . .

458
00:37:12.314 --> 00:37:19.000
<c.p>I can remember going to Lake Charles as I got older.

459
00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:20.216
A teenager.

460
00:37:20.216 --> 00:37:26.294
And I started sewing, so I would go over there.

461
00:37:26.294 --> 00:37:38.451
But we had a fabric place right here in Westlake that we would go to and buy fabric and stuff.

462
00:37:38.451 --> 00:37:54.255
I wasn't really. . . We weren’t really involved that much in the integration part because . . . I guess because we were happy where we were. [Laughs] I mean,

463
00:37:54.255 --> 00:38:04.588
it was a good thing in a way, but the . . . As far as for . . . I don't know.

464
00:38:04.588 --> 00:38:17.353
I just feel like the combining of the schools and all that, I think that the colored children, the black children,

465
00:38:17.353 --> 00:38:31.333
has really been left out in a lot of it, as to the point that they keep on closing our schools, even today.

466
00:38:31.333 --> 00:38:41.059
So, I know when my children went, my daughter was . . . She graduated from Lake Charles High,

467
00:38:41.059 --> 00:38:49.569
because we were in the area where she could go to Lake Charles High.

468
00:38:49.569 --> 00:38:55.039
And my son, he graduated from Lake Charles Boston.

469
00:38:55.039 --> 00:39:07.804
So they had changed over and he was the last class at Boston High, and the first at Lake Charles Boston.

470
00:39:07.804 --> 00:39:12.667
So they, you know . . . they got good educations,

471
00:39:12.667 --> 00:39:23.000
and I know a lot of people didn't want their children going to Lake Charles Boston.

472
00:39:23.000 --> 00:39:32.036
A lot of the black people would send their children to Barbe, LaGrange and all these other schools,

473
00:39:32.036 --> 00:39:43.583
because they felt that they would get a better education, but my granddaughter went to Lake Charles Boston, and we was very proud.

474
00:39:43.583 --> 00:39:48.101
Still is, I'm . . . Still is very proud of her.

475
00:39:48.101 --> 00:39:53.121
She graduated from Lake Charles Boston, from Grambling State University.

476
00:39:53.121 --> 00:40:00.150
While she was at Grambling, she did internship with State Farm in Bloomington, Illinois.

477
00:40:00.150 --> 00:40:04.668
And when she graduated, she got a job there.

478
00:40:04.668 --> 00:40:12.198
And then she stayed there a couple of years, and then she moved to Texas.

479
00:40:12.198 --> 00:40:16.717
She got married and she's still over in Houston.

480
00:40:16.717 --> 00:40:19.227
She's had very good jobs.

481
00:40:19.227 --> 00:40:32.279
And one thing I remember about when she was at Lake Charles Boston, she wanted to take a higher math class, and they didn't offer that.

482
00:40:32.279 --> 00:40:40.312
So she would have to go to LaGrange or Barbe in order to take this class.

483
00:40:40.312 --> 00:40:46.336
And so she . . . They got her in at LaGrange, and she would

484
00:40:46.336 --> 00:40:47.340
<c.p>drive there.

485
00:40:47.340 --> 00:40:50.854
She had to have her own transportation.

486
00:40:50.854 --> 00:40:57.883
It's not like now, the school board have busses with one child on it.

487
00:40:57.883 --> 00:41:01.899
But she had to have her own transportation.

488
00:41:01.899 --> 00:41:12.943
So she drove there for that hour or so, and she remembered that because she told us that the thing . . . The teacher,

489
00:41:12.943 --> 00:41:23.988
he . . . When she first went, he didn't know why she was coming there, because she would be behind all of this students.

490
00:41:23.988 --> 00:41:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why would he say that?

491
00:41:27.000 --> 00:41:33.129
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, because she was coming from Lake Charles Boston, he figured that she, you know, didn't know that much.

492
00:41:33.129 --> 00:41:33.452
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

493
00:41:33.452 --> 00:41:35.710
I don't know anything about that school.

494
00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:37.323
Is it . . . What is it?

495
00:41:37.323 --> 00:41:39.903
Is it just because it's in Lake Charles?

496
00:41:39.903 --> 00:41:40.226
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>What?

497
00:41:40.226 --> 00:41:41.194
<v ARSENEAULT>Lake Charles Boston.

498
00:41:41.194 --> 00:41:43.774
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes, well that was Lake Charles High School.

499
00:41:43.774 --> 00:41:44.419
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

500
00:41:44.419 --> 00:41:44.742
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

501
00:41:44.742 --> 00:41:46.355
And then it changed to . . .

502
00:41:46.355 --> 00:41:47.645
<v ARSENEAULT>And then it changed . . .

503
00:41:47.645 --> 00:41:50.871
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes, when they integrated, it changed to Lake Charles Boston.

504
00:41:50.871 --> 00:41:51.516
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

505
00:41:51.516 --> 00:41:54.097
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Because that was the two schools that combined.

506
00:41:54.097 --> 00:41:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So they changed the name when they integrated.

507
00:41:57.000 --> 00:41:57.450
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

508
00:41:57.450 --> 00:41:57.900
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

509
00:41:57.900 --> 00:42:01.950
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>And so, her teacher . . . the teacher told her that.

510
00:42:01.950 --> 00:42:10.950
That, like . . . he didn't know why she was coming because she was going to be behind all of his students,

511
00:42:10.950 --> 00:42:20.400
and that she was going to really have to work hard if she wanted to catch up and pass that class.

512
00:42:20.400 --> 00:42:30.750
And so, when class ended, she say he called her in, and he told that, you know, that she was his best student.

513
00:42:30.750 --> 00:42:36.150
She . . . And I guess he felt bad for what he had said,

514
00:42:36.150 --> 00:42:43.350
and . . . Because she say when he told her that she told, "Oh, you wait and see."

515
00:42:43.350 --> 00:42:48.300
And I say you know, some students would have not went.

516
00:42:48.300 --> 00:42:50.100
They would have quit.

517
00:42:50.100 --> 00:42:55.050
They would have felt that . . . “Okay, so you feel that way.

518
00:42:55.050 --> 00:43:05.400
That I don't know enough," but I was going to add that she stayed because that was something that she wanted to do,

519
00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:12.150
and all of her jobs and everything has been in accounting, and she's doing very

520
00:43:12.150 --> 00:43:12.600
<c.p>well.

521
00:43:12.600 --> 00:43:18.900
But it just shows sometimes . . . you know, you don't know what a person know.

522
00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:27.000
So . . . they have had . . . I really thank God for my children, my grandchildren, I got great grandchildren now!

523
00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:27.450
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

524
00:43:27.450 --> 00:43:30.600
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[laughs] And so, they're all doing good.

525
00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:34.200
You know, and I just have to be. . .

526
00:43:34.200 --> 00:43:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Must be proud.

527
00:43:36.000 --> 00:43:36.523
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes, yes.

528
00:43:36.523 --> 00:43:37.307
Thankful for that.

529
00:43:37.307 --> 00:43:41.489
So, and, you know. . . I tell them, I say it all started back here, you know.

530
00:43:41.489 --> 00:43:45.148
Because one of my granddaughters, she said, "I don't even know where Mossville is!"

531
00:43:45.148 --> 00:43:50.636
And I said, oh we're going to have to take you because it's not going to be nothing much left there.

532
00:43:50.636 --> 00:43:53.773
So in order for you to at least know where Mossville was.

533
00:43:53.773 --> 00:43:55.602
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you tell them about it?

534
00:43:55.602 --> 00:43:59.261
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I just, you know, mainly tell them that, you know, things that happened.

535
00:43:59.261 --> 00:44:03.705
What . . . because like, I tell them, I said, "We didn't have games and things like y'all do.

536
00:44:03.705 --> 00:44:04.750
We didn't stay inside.

537
00:44:04.750 --> 00:44:06.057
We was always outside playing."

538
00:44:06.057 --> 00:44:08.932
And I say, "We always . . . it was always something to do."

539
00:44:08.932 --> 00:44:11.807
Because like . . . There were times during the summer there like this

540
00:44:11.807 --> 00:44:14.943
<c.p>we would go crawfishing at the track . . . down there by the track.

541
00:44:14.943 --> 00:44:17.818
It would be a group of us all the time and . . .

542
00:44:17.818 --> 00:44:18.864
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there crawfish holes?

543
00:44:18.864 --> 00:44:21.216
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, it would be . . . Yeah, on side the track.

544
00:44:21.216 --> 00:44:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

545
00:44:22.000 --> 00:44:27.454
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, you have a pond, whatever, whatever, the water was there.

546
00:44:27.454 --> 00:44:32.411
So we would get a stick that was strong enough.

547
00:44:32.411 --> 00:44:37.865
We made our own poles, I mean we didn't buy nothing.

548
00:44:37.865 --> 00:44:44.806
So we took our . . . We got a stick, we tied some string on it,

549
00:44:44.806 --> 00:44:50.755
and we would get a piece of meat. . . fat meat, salt meat,

550
00:44:50.755 --> 00:44:57.696
whatever, and we would tie it on the end and we would go crawfishing.

551
00:44:57.696 --> 00:45:07.611
And we would catch crawfish, and we would come back home and our parents would boil it for us, whatever.

552
00:45:07.611 --> 00:45:11.577
This one time . . . Well, we stopped after this.

553
00:45:11.577 --> 00:45:13.561
We didn't go back.

554
00:45:13.561 --> 00:45:20.997
We was . . . It was down there, and a snake got on one of our lines,

555
00:45:20.997 --> 00:45:31.904
and we all just started running, hollering, screaming, running home, and so that was the end of going down there to crawfish.

556
00:45:31.904 --> 00:45:36.862
But we did things, mostly it was in a group.

557
00:45:36.862 --> 00:45:42.315
You know, all of us that was around the same age.

558
00:45:42.315 --> 00:45:50.248
And, you know, it was in this area because I lived not too far from here.

559
00:45:50.248 --> 00:45:57.189
And we had basketball teams, softball teams, baseball teams, and we would go swimming.

560
00:45:57.189 --> 00:46:05.121
So, we did things, but we didn’t have to . . . We didn't buy or spend like now.

561
00:46:05.121 --> 00:46:08.592
Even I would tell them about Christmas.

562
00:46:08.592 --> 00:46:11.566
They’d be wanting all this stuff.

563
00:46:11.566 --> 00:46:15.532
My little granddaughter, she gives me a list.

564
00:46:15.532 --> 00:46:16.028
You

565
00:46:16.028 --> 00:46:19.994
<c.p>pick whichever one you want to give me.

566
00:46:19.994 --> 00:46:23.465
Then she’ll let somebody else pick something.

567
00:46:23.465 --> 00:46:29.414
And I say, you know at Christmas, we would get one gift.

568
00:46:29.414 --> 00:46:33.380
Whatever you wanted, that's what you would get.

569
00:46:33.380 --> 00:46:37.346
And you know, I say we were satisfied.

570
00:46:37.346 --> 00:46:45.775
I say, one year I might get the doll, the next year I might get tea sets.

571
00:46:45.775 --> 00:46:54.203
So, you know, but it was . . . And we all thought that was normal and good, you know,

572
00:46:54.203 --> 00:47:02.631
because that was the way things was done, and now . . . And I remember we got our TV.

573
00:47:02.631 --> 00:47:05.606
We had black and white TV.

574
00:47:05.606 --> 00:47:09.572
Then they came out with the little screen.

575
00:47:09.572 --> 00:47:18.000
Like the rainbow color screen that you could put over it to make it color [Laughs]

576
00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:20.408
<v ARSENEAULT>I did not know about that!

577
00:47:20.408 --> 00:47:20.810
Wow.

578
00:47:20.810 --> 00:47:21.613
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Yeah.

579
00:47:21.613 --> 00:47:26.430
And so when we first got the telephone, we had party lines.

580
00:47:26.430 --> 00:47:28.035
Nobody had private lines.

581
00:47:28.035 --> 00:47:28.437
And . . .

582
00:47:28.437 --> 00:47:30.845
<v ARSENEAULT>So you could eavesdrop on people?

583
00:47:30.845 --> 00:47:32.049
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh yeah, yeah.

584
00:47:32.049 --> 00:47:34.859
Your phone would ring a certain ring.

585
00:47:34.859 --> 00:47:40.077
That's how you know it was for you, you know, for your house.

586
00:47:40.077 --> 00:47:46.901
And so sometimes when people would be on there, you would pick up, and they were talking.

587
00:47:46.901 --> 00:47:50.113
You know, you wouldn't know at that time.

588
00:47:50.113 --> 00:47:55.732
And then sometimes, you would just stay on there instead of hanging up [Laughs].

589
00:47:55.732 --> 00:48:02.155
But yeah, so it was just a lot of things that's so different now, you know.

590
00:48:02.155 --> 00:48:07.373
And the children just don’t realize all the different things that we did.

591
00:48:07.373 --> 00:48:09.782
And we were happy, you know.

592
00:48:09.782 --> 00:48:11.789
We were happy with that.

593
00:48:11.789 --> 00:48:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have a favorite childhood memory?

594
00:48:15.000 --> 00:48:21.055
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I guess it was the year . . . It was Christmas, and I wanted a bicycle,

595
00:48:21.055 --> 00:48:29.532
and so one of my friends told me that he saw Daddy riding my bicycle to my sister's house to hide.

596
00:48:29.532 --> 00:48:33.569
Because at that time, I still believed in Santa Claus.

597
00:48:33.569 --> 00:48:35.183
And I said, "No!"

598
00:48:35.183 --> 00:48:36.394
He said, "Yeah!"

599
00:48:36.394 --> 00:48:40.028
And he told me how it looked and everything.

600
00:48:40.028 --> 00:48:46.083
So when my sister was gone one day, I went over there to her house.

601
00:48:46.083 --> 00:48:48.908
And I looked, I found my bicycle.

602
00:48:48.908 --> 00:48:51.330
And I found my nephew's bicycle.

603
00:48:51.330 --> 00:48:55.367
Everything was all covered up [Laughs] And I told Daddy,

604
00:48:55.367 --> 00:49:02.633
I said "There's no Santa Claus!' And they kept on telling me, "Yes it was, yes it was!"

605
00:49:02.633 --> 00:49:05.459
I say, "But I saw my bicycle!”

606
00:49:05.459 --> 00:49:08.688
So I knew Santa Claus didn't bring it.

607
00:49:08.688 --> 00:49:11.514
So they wanted to still, I guess,

608
00:49:11.514 --> 00:49:20.394
let me have that memory to believe in Santa Claus so that next year I wanted this bride doll that I'd seen.

609
00:49:20.394 --> 00:49:24.835
And I could not find that doll nowheres at the house.

610
00:49:24.835 --> 00:49:25.239
Nowheres.

611
00:49:25.239 --> 00:49:26.046
Nobody's house.

612
00:49:26.046 --> 00:49:29.679
I went to my brother's house, my sister's house.

613
00:49:29.679 --> 00:49:30.486
Everywheres, looking.

614
00:49:30.486 --> 00:49:32.908
And I never did find it.

615
00:49:32.908 --> 00:49:39.771
And so, when I got it, and he said, "Well now, okay, where did this come from?"

616
00:49:39.771 --> 00:49:49.862
And I said, "It wasn't from Santa Claus, but I don't know where you hid it." [Laughs] But then, after that, Christmas wasn't the same.

617
00:49:49.862 --> 00:49:52.688
After I knew it wasn't Santa Claus.

618
00:49:52.688 --> 00:50:01.165
That's why I tell my children to let their kids believe as long as . . . you know, as long as they can.

619
00:50:01.165 --> 00:50:08.028
Even though I now a lot of people think that's like a myth and all that stuff.

620
00:50:08.028 --> 00:50:10.853
But I think, oh they have fun.

621
00:50:10.853 --> 00:50:14.083
You know, when they think it's Santa Claus.

622
00:50:14.083 --> 00:50:20.541
I say when it's no more Santa Claus, then it's like no more Christmas, you know.

623
00:50:20.541 --> 00:50:24.982
<v ARSENEAULT>So that's your happiest and saddest memory at the same time?

624
00:50:24.982 --> 00:50:26.193
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I think so.

625
00:50:26.193 --> 00:50:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Combined?

626
00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:28.032
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Yeah.

627
00:50:28.032 --> 00:50:28.548
Yeah.

628
00:50:28.548 --> 00:50:30.097
<v ARSENEAULT>So many questions.

629
00:50:30.097 --> 00:50:30.613
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs]

630
00:50:30.613 --> 00:50:35.258
<v ARSENEAULT>So your dad was one of the community leaders?

631
00:50:35.258 --> 00:50:35.774
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

632
00:50:35.774 --> 00:50:40.419
<v ARSENEAULT>Could you talk a little bit more about him?

633
00:50:40.419 --> 00:50:44.548
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, Uncle Josh was the leader . . . Josh Rigmaiden.

634
00:50:44.548 --> 00:50:47.129
That's the swimming pool name.

635
00:50:47.129 --> 00:50:48.161
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh Okay.

636
00:50:48.161 --> 00:50:50.742
So this is Jacob Rigmaiden . . .

637
00:50:50.742 --> 00:50:52.806
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes, and that's Josh.

638
00:50:52.806 --> 00:50:55.387
<v ARSENEAULT>And that's . . . and they're brothers.

639
00:50:55.387 --> 00:50:57.452
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>My brother's the pavilion.

640
00:50:57.452 --> 00:50:59.000
That's James.

641
00:50:59.000 --> 00:50:59.356
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

642
00:50:59.356 --> 00:51:01.494
Okay, so Josh is your dad . . .

643
00:51:01.494 --> 00:51:01.851
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Jacob.

644
00:51:01.851 --> 00:51:02.920
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay Jacob . . . Rigmaiden.

645
00:51:02.920 --> 00:51:04.345
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Jacob is my dad.

646
00:51:04.345 --> 00:51:05.770
And Josh, my uncle.

647
00:51:05.770 --> 00:51:07.908
<v ARSENEAULT>Josh, your uncle, is the pool.

648
00:51:07.908 --> 00:51:08.264
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[agrees]

649
00:51:08.264 --> 00:51:08.977
<v ARSENEAULT>And James . . .

650
00:51:08.977 --> 00:51:09.690
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>My brother.

651
00:51:09.690 --> 00:51:11.471
<v ARSENEAULT>Your brother is the pavilion.

652
00:51:11.471 --> 00:51:11.828
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

653
00:51:11.828 --> 00:51:12.897
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, got it.

654
00:51:12.897 --> 00:51:13.966
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[laughs] Yeah, okay.

655
00:51:13.966 --> 00:51:16.816
Well, Uncle Josh was really . . . well he was

656
00:51:16.816 --> 00:51:18.241
<c.p>the mayor of Mossville.

657
00:51:18.241 --> 00:51:22.161
That's what everybody . . . yeah, everybody called him the mayor of Mossville.

658
00:51:22.161 --> 00:51:23.586
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he voted, or . . . ?

659
00:51:23.586 --> 00:51:28.931
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I don't think so, I think it's just something that the people just made him.

660
00:51:28.931 --> 00:51:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

661
00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:35.180
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Because he would help them, and you know, anything that they needed.

662
00:51:35.180 --> 00:51:36.907
Like he was . . . Well,

663
00:51:36.907 --> 00:51:52.880
I guess Daddy like took his place when he died because he was like in with the politics as far as for the people to help the people in Mossville who was best . . . best served them and,

664
00:51:52.880 --> 00:52:00.219
you know, if they got in trouble or anything, he would go to talk to the sheriff,

665
00:52:00.219 --> 00:52:07.126
to the D.A., and, you know, help them to get out of the trouble or whatever.

666
00:52:07.126 --> 00:52:11.443
And so, this is what, you know, Uncle Josh did.

667
00:52:11.443 --> 00:52:20.077
And then when Uncle Josh died, well, they said Daddy was the mayor, but Dad say he wasn't the mayor.

668
00:52:20.077 --> 00:52:23.530
They never . . . There wasn't no election or nothing.

669
00:52:23.530 --> 00:52:30.869
It was just the people that, you know, just felt that way because they would help them.

670
00:52:30.869 --> 00:52:39.503
You know, whatever it was that they needed . . . financial, spiritual, whatever type of help, they would do that for them.

671
00:52:39.503 --> 00:52:47.273
So I guess that was just . . . I guess that was just in they blood to do that [Laughs].

672
00:52:47.273 --> 00:52:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They were leaders.

673
00:52:49.000 --> 00:52:49.945
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, yeah.

674
00:52:49.945 --> 00:52:51.835
They were always . . . yeah.

675
00:52:51.835 --> 00:52:59.866
But Daddy didn't believe, I guess, in the girls doing anything as far as for that way,

676
00:52:59.866 --> 00:53:10.260
and he only had one son and four girls, so I mean . . . you know, it wasn't nothing much that we did [Laughs].

677
00:53:10.260 --> 00:53:13.567
<v ARSENEAULT>What did he expect y'all to do?

678
00:53:13.567 --> 00:53:19.709
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I mean, you know, he didn't want us to be in politics.

679
00:53:19.709 --> 00:53:30.102
He didn't, you know . . . just the things when the community . . . When the people would have their meetings and things would go on,

680
00:53:30.102 --> 00:53:31.520
we weren't included.

681
00:53:31.520 --> 00:53:40.024
And I guess it was just the thing of that the male ran things, and the females didn't.

682
00:53:40.024 --> 00:53:46.638
I feel like that was a lot of it for how he was concerned.

683
00:53:46.638 --> 00:53:57.504
And I guess a lot was protecting the daughters and everything from anything that might would come up or . . . we were like protected.

684
00:53:57.504 --> 00:54:00.811
Because that was the way he is.

685
00:54:00.811 --> 00:54:04.591
That was the way he did at home.

686
00:54:04.591 --> 00:54:08.370
He would never . . . We never got a whipping.

687
00:54:08.370 --> 00:54:09.787
The girls didn't.

688
00:54:09.787 --> 00:54:22.071
James did [Laughs], but the girls didn't because he just felt that I guess that if he would whip us or something, he might hurt us.

689
00:54:22.071 --> 00:54:27.268
So all the whipping and the punishment fell on my mother.

690
00:54:27.268 --> 00:54:30.575
She very seldom would do anything either.

691
00:54:30.575 --> 00:54:37.661
So we pretty much got away with things. [Laughs] I mean, it wasn't too much.

692
00:54:37.661 --> 00:54:40.496
I mean we would get punished.

693
00:54:40.496 --> 00:54:46.165
But not like my friends . . . as much as my friends would, so . . .

694
00:54:46.165 --> 00:54:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How would y'all get punished?

695
00:54:49.000 --> 00:54:52.821
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well . . . we would get whipped with the switch.

696
00:54:52.821 --> 00:54:58.554
Momma would make us go get a switch and would whip us.

697
00:54:58.554 --> 00:55:03.330
And one time I had, me and my nephew was

698
00:55:03.330 --> 00:55:13.362
<c.p>playing bingo, and he didn't want to play anymore, so he messed up the board, and I said a curse word.

699
00:55:13.362 --> 00:55:20.527
And so Momma made me go get the switch, and she gave me three licks.

700
00:55:20.527 --> 00:55:22.438
I'll never forget that.

701
00:55:22.438 --> 00:55:28.170
She gave me three licks, and I cried and cried and cried.

702
00:55:28.170 --> 00:55:35.812
And I guess it hurt me more that she whipped me than the licks, you know.

703
00:55:35.812 --> 00:55:41.545
But I mean, it was just. . . it hurt my feelings so much,

704
00:55:41.545 --> 00:55:46.799
but . . . that was . . . it wasn't . . . we never really, say, get punished.

705
00:55:46.799 --> 00:55:52.054
It wasn't punishment like, "I'm going to take this from you."

706
00:55:52.054 --> 00:55:56.830
It wasn't none of that stuff like it is today.

707
00:55:56.830 --> 00:56:04.473
But you would get your whipping and, you know . . . and then everything would be alright [Laughs].

708
00:56:04.473 --> 00:56:06.384
<v ARSENEAULT>And that was that.

709
00:56:06.384 --> 00:56:07.817
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Yeah, so. . .

710
00:56:07.817 --> 00:56:12.116
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, goodness. . . So more about like the community leaders.

711
00:56:12.116 --> 00:56:22.147
I'm just curious because people, whenever we talk to them, they do mention that Josh Rigmaiden was like a community leader.

712
00:56:22.147 --> 00:56:25.969
So what kind of things did he do?

713
00:56:25.969 --> 00:56:36.000
He helped people, but was he always like in . . . People always over at your house, or how did that work?

714
00:56:36.000 --> 00:56:45.067
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, not always over at the house, but if they needed something or whatever, they would come.

715
00:56:45.067 --> 00:56:54.667
But it was . . . they would just . . . you know, I guess like assist them if they got into trouble.

716
00:56:54.667 --> 00:57:01.067
They would go to the district attorney and talk for a minute.

717
00:57:01.067 --> 00:57:11.733
Try to help them if they were going to serve time or anything, let it be as least as possible.

718
00:57:11.733 --> 00:57:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any as . . . in particular?

719
00:57:16.000 --> 00:57:20.674
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, I mean I'm not going to say anything.

720
00:57:20.674 --> 00:57:21.713
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

721
00:57:21.713 --> 00:57:25.348
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, I don't want to say anything.

722
00:57:25.348 --> 00:57:34.696
But they . . . and you know, they would talk . . . I know they were friends with Ham Reed, the sheriff.

723
00:57:34.696 --> 00:57:35.735
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

724
00:57:35.735 --> 00:57:43.006
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, and so you know . . . they got special friends with the politicians and things.

725
00:57:43.006 --> 00:57:46.641
And so they were able to help.

726
00:57:46.641 --> 00:57:54.431
If it was something they couldn't do, they knew where to go to get assistance.

727
00:57:54.431 --> 00:58:01.702
Like, if some of the children needed like . . . well they call it scholarship money,

728
00:58:01.702 --> 00:58:09.492
but at that time some of the politicians would give them money to go to,

729
00:58:09.492 --> 00:58:21.436
say McNeese, they would give them a certain amount like a hundred dollars or something, and back then that was good, you know.

730
00:58:21.436 --> 00:58:31.823
So they would do all these different type of things to, you know, just to help the community, the people.

731
00:58:31.823 --> 00:58:39.094
And they the ones that talked to get these things that we have here.

732
00:58:39.094 --> 00:58:44.807
They were diligent to . . . Because, I mean we didn't have anything.

733
00:58:44.807 --> 00:58:50.000
And to get, first we got the swimming pool.

734
00:58:50.000 --> 00:58:56.801
And they had to . . . you know, I mean just stay in conference talking to people,

735
00:58:56.801 --> 00:59:02.696
and you know, getting them to where they would build that for us.

736
00:59:02.696 --> 00:59:05.870
And even just this, the recreation center.

737
00:59:05.870 --> 00:59:07.230
All of that.

738
00:59:07.230 --> 00:59:08.137
The school.

739
00:59:08.137 --> 00:59:13.124
All of that was just things that they had to diligently

740
00:59:13.124 --> 00:59:19.925
<c.p>work, and you know, keep letting the people know that, you know, we needed this.

741
00:59:19.925 --> 00:59:23.099
And we needed, you know, it here.

742
00:59:23.099 --> 00:59:25.366
Because we didn't have anything.

743
00:59:25.366 --> 00:59:29.447
We didn’t have, you know, the swimming pool, nothing.

744
00:59:29.447 --> 00:59:33.528
Even though other areas, Sulphur, Westlake, they had that.

745
00:59:33.528 --> 00:59:39.422
So the leaders wanted for the Mossville children to have the same things,

746
00:59:39.422 --> 00:59:45.317
and so this was most of their . . . how diligent they had to work.

747
00:59:45.317 --> 00:59:48.944
It's not like it is today, you know.

748
00:59:48.944 --> 00:59:53.025
You vote on it, and okay, you got it.

749
00:59:53.025 --> 01:00:03.000
It wasn't like that because they had to go through a lot of different stuff to get these things for us.

750
01:00:03.000 --> 01:00:07.268
<v WILLCOX>We've been talking for about an hour, just to let you know.

751
01:00:07.268 --> 01:00:07.624
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Really?

752
01:00:07.624 --> 01:00:08.691
Oh my goodness!

753
01:00:08.691 --> 01:00:10.826
<v ARSENEAULT>I know, it goes by fast.

754
01:00:10.826 --> 01:00:14.027
You want to go a little longer, you good?

755
01:00:14.027 --> 01:00:15.094
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, we can.

756
01:00:15.094 --> 01:00:15.450
Yeah.

757
01:00:15.450 --> 01:00:15.805
Okay.

758
01:00:15.805 --> 01:00:21.141
<v ARSENEAULT>I want to hear a little bit more about Mossville when you were growing up.

759
01:00:21.141 --> 01:00:27.544
I'm also curious . . . Let me ask this first, since you were talking about your uncle and your dad.

760
01:00:27.544 --> 01:00:30.389
What did they talk about World War Two?

761
01:00:30.389 --> 01:00:32.523
What did they say about that?

762
01:00:32.523 --> 01:00:34.302
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>They weren't in the war.

763
01:00:34.302 --> 01:00:34.658
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

764
01:00:34.658 --> 01:00:37.148
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>My father wasn't military, neither Uncle Josh.

765
01:00:37.148 --> 01:00:40.349
And neither one of them went in the military.

766
01:00:40.349 --> 01:00:48.174
I think when they had one war, Daddy was too young, and when they had the other one, he was too old.

767
01:00:48.174 --> 01:00:50.309
So he never got to go.

768
01:00:50.309 --> 01:00:56.000
My brother was in the army, but he wasn't . . . It wasn't during a war time.

769
01:00:56.000 --> 01:01:00.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone in the community ever talk about it, or . . .?

770
01:01:00.600 --> 01:01:01.060
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No.

771
01:01:01.060 --> 01:01:01.980
<v ARSENEAULT>Wasn't a . . .

772
01:01:01.980 --> 01:01:02.440
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No.

773
01:01:02.440 --> 01:01:05.200
<v ARSENEAULT>No stories about it or anything?

774
01:01:05.200 --> 01:01:08.420
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, I don't know anything about . . . yeah.

775
01:01:08.420 --> 01:01:10.720
They never talk about that.

776
01:01:10.720 --> 01:01:14.860
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, well growing up . . . what was the community like?

777
01:01:14.860 --> 01:01:16.700
Could you describe it?

778
01:01:16.700 --> 01:01:19.000
Like its physical appearance.

779
01:01:19.000 --> 01:01:24.076
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>We only had . . . It was just a few houses here.

780
01:01:24.076 --> 01:01:31.181
I guess between here and Prater Road . . . it may have been like eight houses?

781
01:01:31.181 --> 01:01:34.227
And that would be on this

782
01:01:34.227 --> 01:01:36.257
<c.p>side of the street.

783
01:01:36.257 --> 01:01:40.825
On that side the street it was even less.

784
01:01:40.825 --> 01:01:46.916
It just, you know, it wasn't . . . Because it wasn't many of us.

785
01:01:46.916 --> 01:01:47.931
You know.

786
01:01:47.931 --> 01:01:53.514
But a lot of people lived back down streets, you know.

787
01:01:53.514 --> 01:01:58.082
Where what we call dirt roads [Laughs] . . . gravel roads.

788
01:01:58.082 --> 01:02:03.158
They lived off of the Old Spanish Trail, you know.

789
01:02:03.158 --> 01:02:07.218
There were houses, but they were far apart.

790
01:02:07.218 --> 01:02:08.741
It wasn't many.

791
01:02:08.741 --> 01:02:16.862
And those people, you would have to go a long ways to go to their home.

792
01:02:16.862 --> 01:02:28.536
And I know, when Momma and Daddy I guess first married, they used to live somewheres up from Prater Road across the street,

793
01:02:28.536 --> 01:02:38.687
back down some dirt road because Momma used to say how far they had to walk to get to church.

794
01:02:38.687 --> 01:02:45.285
Because we went to the Morning Star over here past the railroad tracks.

795
01:02:45.285 --> 01:02:51.883
And they lived past Prater Road and back up in the woods somewheres,

796
01:02:51.883 --> 01:02:59.497
and they would have to walk because at that time they didn't have a car.

797
01:02:59.497 --> 01:03:05.587
And she would walk with . . . She had the three children at first.

798
01:03:05.587 --> 01:03:07.618
And they would walk.

799
01:03:07.618 --> 01:03:09.648
Between my [brother] . . . James.

800
01:03:09.648 --> 01:03:13.708
Then we had Ida Mae and then Deannie.

801
01:03:13.708 --> 01:03:18.784
And between Deannie and Esther it's like a seven-year span.

802
01:03:18.784 --> 01:03:24.367
So Esther and then me, we were like separate from them.

803
01:03:24.367 --> 01:03:33.503
I mean because they were like almost grown when we were [born] . . . when we was doing things here.

804
01:03:33.503 --> 01:03:37.056
They were already had done left home.

805
01:03:37.056 --> 01:03:42.639
So it was . . . we was . . . It was like two different families.

806
01:03:42.639 --> 01:03:48.222
Like now, nowadays it's kids fifteen years apart, you know [Laughs].

807
01:03:48.222 --> 01:03:59.389
But back then, seven years, that was a long time for children to . . . for you to start having another family as such.

808
01:03:59.389 --> 01:04:03.449
But, yeah, it was mostly just vacant lots.

809
01:04:03.449 --> 01:04:05.987
Well, a lot of trees.

810
01:04:05.987 --> 01:04:13.093
You know, it was a lot of trees here, and it wasn't much else.

811
01:04:13.093 --> 01:04:20.706
You'd have to . . . We would walk distances to get to wherever we wanted to go.

812
01:04:20.706 --> 01:04:28.320
I guess that's why we mostly just stayed within our little community, our little area.

813
01:04:28.320 --> 01:04:39.486
And we played and when we got old enough to come to school, well then, we would play with all the children.

814
01:04:39.486 --> 01:04:50.652
Because the children from way down the other side of Prater Road, you know, where we all came to the same school.

815
01:04:50.652 --> 01:04:56.235
So we got to know them and to be friends with

816
01:04:56.235 --> 01:04:56.743
<c.p>them.

817
01:04:56.743 --> 01:05:00.803
But back then, we were all like kin.

818
01:05:00.803 --> 01:05:14.000
I mean, so we knew each other, but we didn't play with each other that much because of the distance of the houses and things.

819
01:05:14.000 --> 01:05:17.356
<v ARSENEAULT>When did it start getting more . . .

820
01:05:17.356 --> 01:05:21.270
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>About 1954, when they did the school.

821
01:05:21.270 --> 01:05:24.067
<v ARSENEAULT>That's right, you said that.

822
01:05:24.067 --> 01:05:24.626
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

823
01:05:24.626 --> 01:05:33.015
That's when they started with all the different subdivisions and people started moving out here.

824
01:05:33.015 --> 01:05:40.844
They . . . you know, I don't know why, but they wanted to come out here.

825
01:05:40.844 --> 01:05:53.148
And it was a lot of land, so someone would buy up a big plot of land and build houses and things,

826
01:05:53.148 --> 01:06:04.893
so it was . . . I guess they decided they would come a little further west than Westlake, and we were it. [Laughs]

827
01:06:04.893 --> 01:06:08.807
<v ARSENEAULT>What were the weddings like in Mossville?

828
01:06:08.807 --> 01:06:13.841
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Most of the weddings were done at the house.

829
01:06:13.841 --> 01:06:22.230
My sister's wedding . . . it was done on the front porch [Laughs] and Daddy married them,

830
01:06:22.230 --> 01:06:29.500
and . . . but they, you know, it was the same like the weddings today.

831
01:06:29.500 --> 01:06:39.007
They had, you know, you had your bride, she had her dress, bridesmaids, they had their dresses.

832
01:06:39.007 --> 01:06:46.278
And to have the food mainly was like cake and punch or whatever.

833
01:06:46.278 --> 01:06:54.107
It wasn't no elaborate meals and all that stuff as to what they had.

834
01:06:54.107 --> 01:06:58.022
But I don't really remember her wedding.

835
01:06:58.022 --> 01:07:03.615
I remember the pictures, and I remember everybody talking about

836
01:07:03.615 --> 01:07:04.174
<c.p>it.

837
01:07:04.174 --> 01:07:10.885
So that's how I can talk about it and know, you know.

838
01:07:10.885 --> 01:07:17.037
Hers was the only wedding that I remember in my family.

839
01:07:17.037 --> 01:07:24.867
My brother, he married at the house, but they didn't have a big wedding.

840
01:07:24.867 --> 01:07:30.459
They just had a small wedding, and . . . let me see.

841
01:07:30.459 --> 01:07:34.933
And Deanie, I think she married in California.

842
01:07:34.933 --> 01:07:45.000
But well, me and Esther married in Lake Charles, so we were different from the rest [Laughs].

843
01:07:45.000 --> 01:07:49.406
<v ARSENEAULT>When did you . . . you have family in California?

844
01:07:49.406 --> 01:07:49.957
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

845
01:07:49.957 --> 01:07:52.710
<v ARSENEAULT>When did they move there?

846
01:07:52.710 --> 01:07:53.261
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh.

847
01:07:53.261 --> 01:07:56.014
<v ARSENEAULT>Hey Charlotte, [you see her?]?

848
01:07:56.014 --> 01:07:59.870
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>In the early . . . nineteen hundreds I guess.

849
01:07:59.870 --> 01:08:03.174
They . . . because they had been there.

850
01:08:03.174 --> 01:08:07.029
When I was little they were there.

851
01:08:07.029 --> 01:08:11.435
That was Daddy's nieces, nephews, lived out there.

852
01:08:11.435 --> 01:08:15.841
And one of his uncles lived out there.

853
01:08:15.841 --> 01:08:21.899
Because at that time, when he went to California, he . . . Well,

854
01:08:21.899 --> 01:08:27.406
my father and . . . Well, the Rigmaidens were real light-skinned people.

855
01:08:27.406 --> 01:08:31.812
And they could pass for white, you know.

856
01:08:31.812 --> 01:08:33.464
My daddy could.

857
01:08:33.464 --> 01:08:37.319
People thought he was white at times.

858
01:08:37.319 --> 01:08:42.275
And so my uncle, when he went to California,

859
01:08:42.275 --> 01:08:54.391
he . . . They had a story about him in Ebony Magazine that he said “I live two lives."' He would work and everything,

860
01:08:54.391 --> 01:08:57.145
and there he was white.

861
01:08:57.145 --> 01:09:02.101
And when he would come home, he was black.

862
01:09:02.101 --> 01:09:04.855
And his wife was dark.

863
01:09:04.855 --> 01:09:07.609
And so when anything . . . Anybody

864
01:09:07.609 --> 01:09:14.768
<c.p>from his job would come to his house, she was like his maid.

865
01:09:14.768 --> 01:09:19.725
So and he, you know, he wrote his story.

866
01:09:19.725 --> 01:09:22.478
His life story on that.

867
01:09:22.478 --> 01:09:27.435
And that's what I remember, you know, about him.

868
01:09:27.435 --> 01:09:29.638
That he did that.

869
01:09:29.638 --> 01:09:37.899
And he did that so, you know, that they could advance, they could have things.

870
01:09:37.899 --> 01:09:46.710
So that's, you know, sort of a lot of the Rigmaidens, they could pass for white.

871
01:09:46.710 --> 01:09:49.464
Especially that . . . the older group.

872
01:09:49.464 --> 01:09:58.275
My mother was dark, so I'm the darkest . . . I was the darkest one of the children.

873
01:09:58.275 --> 01:10:04.884
And so . . . But there were times when people thought you know, here,

874
01:10:04.884 --> 01:10:13.696
I mean not here in Mossville, because they all knew him, but like Westlake, Lake Charles.

875
01:10:13.696 --> 01:10:17.000
They took him for white.

876
01:10:17.000 --> 01:10:17.514
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees]

877
01:10:17.514 --> 01:10:24.191
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>There was one time they had told me about at the grocery store.

878
01:10:24.191 --> 01:10:33.436
Daddy had put . . . was putting the groceries up on the counter, and Momma was standing behind the basket.

879
01:10:33.436 --> 01:10:43.708
And the clerk asked him where did his groceries stop and hers start [Laugh], and he say, "It's all together."

880
01:10:43.708 --> 01:10:53.981
You know, so it was times when, you know, people thought that he was white, and I know Uncle Josh,

881
01:10:53.981 --> 01:11:01.685
you know, all them they . . . I think they had maybe one brother that was dark.

882
01:11:01.685 --> 01:11:11.444
I guess it's always one in the family [Laughs] you know, that was dark that took after somebody else.

883
01:11:11.444 --> 01:11:21.716
But yeah, most of them was where they could have, yeah they could've been . . . they could have passed either way.

884
01:11:21.716 --> 01:11:26.339
<v ARSENEAULT>What about . . . So was everyone invited to the weddings?

885
01:11:26.339 --> 01:11:28.393
Like the whole community?

886
01:11:28.393 --> 01:11:28.907
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

887
01:11:28.907 --> 01:11:29.934
Oh, yeah.

888
01:11:29.934 --> 01:11:30.447
Everybody.

889
01:11:30.447 --> 01:11:32.502
Yeah, everybody would come.

890
01:11:32.502 --> 01:11:33.016
That

891
01:11:33.016 --> 01:11:41.747
<c.p>was . . . Because mainly it would be the people in the weddings, they always people that lived here.

892
01:11:41.747 --> 01:11:43.802
I mean, you know.

893
01:11:43.802 --> 01:11:45.856
And let me see.

894
01:11:45.856 --> 01:11:50.992
I think she had about six girls in her wedding.

895
01:11:50.992 --> 01:11:55.615
And so they were all family, friends, you know.

896
01:11:55.615 --> 01:11:56.642
But, yeah.

897
01:11:56.642 --> 01:12:06.401
Everybody would be invited, you know, because at that time, it wasn't no lot of people here, so . . . yeah.

898
01:12:06.401 --> 01:12:11.023
And whatever we did, most everything was done together.

899
01:12:11.023 --> 01:12:23.864
Like if somebody would kill a hog or something, others would come, you know, and get food and it was just a thing they did.

900
01:12:23.864 --> 01:12:29.000
It was just. . . I don't know, it's just good.

901
01:12:29.000 --> 01:12:30.521
<v ARSENEAULT>What about funerals?

902
01:12:30.521 --> 01:12:31.027
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Funerals?

903
01:12:31.027 --> 01:12:34.575
<v ARSENEAULT>Tell us a little bit about those.

904
01:12:34.575 --> 01:12:35.082
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh.

905
01:12:35.082 --> 01:12:42.685
Coming up . . . Uncle Josh's was the only one that I went to as a child.

906
01:12:42.685 --> 01:12:49.781
Me and Della Ann was his daughter [Frank and Della Ann the same age].

907
01:12:49.781 --> 01:12:53.329
I know y'all had talked to her.

908
01:12:53.329 --> 01:13:00.425
I don't know if y'all interviewed her yet, but she's a Dodson now and

909
01:13:00.425 --> 01:13:02.452
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, Ms. Della Dodson.

910
01:13:02.452 --> 01:13:02.959
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

911
01:13:02.959 --> 01:13:06.000
Yeah, well we first cousins.

912
01:13:06.000 --> 01:13:07.027
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

913
01:13:07.027 --> 01:13:11.135
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, and so Uncle Josh was her daddy.

914
01:13:11.135 --> 01:13:21.919
And so I remember going to his funeral, and it was just many people there because he knew so many people.

915
01:13:21.919 --> 01:13:25.514
It was whites, blacks, I mean everybody.

916
01:13:25.514 --> 01:13:30.135
All the people from here, and just all around.

917
01:13:30.135 --> 01:13:38.351
Because by him being a minister . . . And most of his churches, too, they weren't right here.

918
01:13:38.351 --> 01:13:40.919
He had churches other places.

919
01:13:40.919 --> 01:13:53.757
And so everybody would come from there because he was like what they call the president of the district, the district that they were in.

920
01:13:53.757 --> 01:13:55.811
So everybody would come.

921
01:13:55.811 --> 01:14:01.459
And all of the ministers, everything, they all had to talk.

922
01:14:01.459 --> 01:14:10.703
So funerals then was like two hours or longer because it was no time limit at that time.

923
01:14:10.703 --> 01:14:14.811
Now they put a time limit on you.

924
01:14:14.811 --> 01:14:22.000
So everybody would have to talk and it was just so many people.

925
01:14:22.000 --> 01:14:28.086
That was the same way, still was, with when my daddy died.

926
01:14:28.086 --> 01:14:36.200
Because he was the bishop, he was the head of the district also when he died.

927
01:14:36.200 --> 01:14:40.257
And so that's the way it was then.

928
01:14:40.257 --> 01:14:48.879
All the ministers . . . I mean it might be like twenty ministers, and they all had to talk.

929
01:14:48.879 --> 01:14:52.936
I mean because you couldn’t leave nobody out.

930
01:14:52.936 --> 01:15:01.557
And after the funerals, they would have food for the people, same way as they do now.

931
01:15:01.557 --> 01:15:11.193
You would talk and . . . Because a lot of times you would . . . You hadn't seen them in a long time.

932
01:15:11.193 --> 01:15:13.729
Not until there's a funeral.

933
01:15:13.729 --> 01:15:21.843
So, you know, you get to talk to the people you hadn't talked to in years.

934
01:15:21.843 --> 01:15:26.914
That was the worst thing, how long they would last.

935
01:15:26.914 --> 01:15:36.550
And my brother said put a limit on the pastors when it was Daddy . . . when it was Daddy's funeral.

936
01:15:36.550 --> 01:15:43.650
But they told him they couldn't go by that three minutes or whatever [Laughs].

937
01:15:43.650 --> 01:15:51.257
They say because of who he was, they had things that they wanted to say.

938
01:15:51.257 --> 01:15:57.343
And they couldn't say it in three minutes, so [?] he got mad.

939
01:15:57.343 --> 01:16:00.386
But it was . . . it's just thing.

940
01:16:00.386 --> 01:16:10.021
A lot of times you do things because of who somebody was, not for what you want to do.

941
01:16:10.021 --> 01:16:18.136
There are times when you just have to accept things because Daddy, too, was well known.

942
01:16:18.136 --> 01:16:24.729
And the people, you know, Lake Charles and all of his church members

943
01:16:24.729 --> 01:16:30.814
<c.p>from Texas and all around came by him being over the district.

944
01:16:30.814 --> 01:16:35.886
And our district consisted of Louisiana and Texas . . . and California.

945
01:16:35.886 --> 01:16:41.464
And so people would come from all these areas, you know.

946
01:16:41.464 --> 01:16:44.507
And so you couldn't slight nobody.

947
01:16:44.507 --> 01:16:46.536
That was the thing.

948
01:16:46.536 --> 01:16:55.664
You couldn't slight any of them, because they were all important, and they all felt Daddy was important.

949
01:16:55.664 --> 01:17:00.736
So . . . and Daddy had, you know, just like Uncle Josh.

950
01:17:00.736 --> 01:17:02.257
They had friends.

951
01:17:02.257 --> 01:17:06.314
They had made friends, you know, with politicians,

952
01:17:06.314 --> 01:17:17.471
with owners of stores and different things because what's now Billy Navarre [car dealership], that used to be Glen Overman in Sulphur.

953
01:17:17.471 --> 01:17:22.543
And he was a pallbearer in Daddy . . . at Daddy's funeral.

954
01:17:22.543 --> 01:17:29.136
Daddy would buy cars from him, and they just got to be friends.

955
01:17:29.136 --> 01:17:32.179
So it was just, you know.

956
01:17:32.179 --> 01:17:41.307
The funerals that I've been to way back or years back, that was the way that they were.

957
01:17:41.307 --> 01:17:43.843
You know, you expected that.

958
01:17:43.843 --> 01:17:50.943
You knew you were going to be here . . . there a long time, you know.

959
01:17:50.943 --> 01:17:55.000
So you just have to come prepared.

960
01:17:55.000 --> 01:18:01.158
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess we can . . . Would you mind if we scheduled a follow-up interview?

961
01:18:01.158 --> 01:18:05.421
I didn't get to nearly any of my questions.

962
01:18:05.421 --> 01:18:06.368
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh, okay.

963
01:18:06.368 --> 01:18:08.263
That would be good.

964
01:18:08.263 --> 01:18:11.105
<v ARSENEAULT>One last question before we go.

965
01:18:11.105 --> 01:18:11.579
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Okay.

966
01:18:11.579 --> 01:18:15.842
<v ARSENEAULT>I was curious about any healers in the area.

967
01:18:15.842 --> 01:18:18.684
Are there any healers in Mossville?

968
01:18:18.684 --> 01:18:22.947
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, the only . . . I don't know of any personally.

969
01:18:22.947 --> 01:18:29.579
But I knew that my great . . . great [Laughs] grandfather, I think, was a healer.

970
01:18:29.579 --> 01:18:30.526
Luke Rigmaiden.

971
01:18:30.526 --> 01:18:41.895
And all I know of is what I was told, and that people would come to him and I don't know what he did,

972
01:18:41.895 --> 01:18:48.526
what type of . . . what . . . if he had herbs or . . . you know, I don't know.

973
01:18:48.526 --> 01:18:51.368
I don't know what he did.

974
01:18:51.368 --> 01:18:56.579
But I knew that they said like on Sundays, you know,

975
01:18:56.579 --> 01:19:05.105
I mean people just be all at his house from Texas, everywheres, to be come to be healed.

976
01:19:05.105 --> 01:19:12.211
But I don’t know of anybody personally that . . . here that, you know, was into healing.

977
01:19:12.211 --> 01:19:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was your . . . you said a great-great-great grandfather?

978
01:19:16.000 --> 01:19:16.256
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[agrees]

979
01:19:16.256 --> 01:19:17.538
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you born with it?

980
01:19:17.538 --> 01:19:19.590
Or was it something you were born with?

981
01:19:19.590 --> 01:19:21.897
Or like how did you know you're a healer?

982
01:19:21.897 --> 01:19:23.179
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh, I don't know. [laughs].

983
01:19:23.179 --> 01:19:24.462
<v ARSENEAULT>They didn't talk about it?

984
01:19:24.462 --> 01:19:26.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I don't know, no. [Laughs]

985
01:19:26.000 --> 01:19:28.172
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, I'm just curious.

986
01:19:28.172 --> 01:19:30.344
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, I don't know.

987
01:19:30.344 --> 01:19:33.058
Because it wasn't handed down.

988
01:19:33.058 --> 01:19:39.031
I don't know of nobody after him . . . you know, that healed.

989
01:19:39.031 --> 01:19:45.546
I know that we believed in healing in our church, you know.

990
01:19:45.546 --> 01:19:49.890
The Christ Sanctified Holy Church believed in healing.

991
01:19:49.890 --> 01:19:58.034
And there were . . . I know that they healed a lady in Jennings [Louisiana] one year.

992
01:19:58.034 --> 01:19:59.663
She couldn't walk.

993
01:19:59.663 --> 01:20:07.807
And they, all the ministers, all the pastors came, and they laid hands on her,

994
01:20:07.807 --> 01:20:17.037
and they prayed, and this went for a long time, and the woman was able to walk.

995
01:20:17.037 --> 01:20:22.466
And but I guess we don't really call that healing.

996
01:20:22.466 --> 01:20:30.067
I . . . We more or less, I guess, call that spiritual healing . . . that God healed.

997
01:20:30.067 --> 01:20:32.782
He just worked through them.

998
01:20:32.782 --> 01:20:42.012
But it's . . . This was the only thing that I know of for myself that someone was healed.

999
01:20:42.012 --> 01:20:53.414
And I know other people's been prayed for and they’ve laid hands on, and but you know, they didn't get healed.

1000
01:20:53.414 --> 01:20:56.672
So, you know, I don't know.

1001
01:20:56.672 --> 01:21:02.644
I don't know, you know what was the difference back then.

1002
01:21:02.644 --> 01:21:14.589
That's why I say I don't know if he . . . if they had some kind of medication, you know, if you gave them.

1003
01:21:14.589 --> 01:21:28.163
Because I'm sure it was something that he made, you know, if he healed them that way, and I don't know if he was spiritual.

1004
01:21:28.163 --> 01:21:31.963
I don't know what it was then.

1005
01:21:31.963 --> 01:21:40.650
But I do know that, you know, the Christ Sanctified Holy Church do believe in healing.

1006
01:21:40.650 --> 01:21:41.736
You know.

1007
01:21:41.736 --> 01:21:52.052
It's not . . . We don't even have any really in this area anymore . . . but they still have them in Texas,

1008
01:21:52.052 --> 01:21:54.224
in the Texas district.

1009
01:21:54.224 --> 01:21:59.110
So, but that was as far as I know.

1010
01:21:59.110 --> 01:22:05.626
But it wasn't like you could just go up and heal somebody.

1011
01:22:05.626 --> 01:22:10.512
You had to lay hands on them with oil.

1012
01:22:10.512 --> 01:22:13.770
I know they did the oil.

1013
01:22:13.770 --> 01:22:14.856
And prayer.

1014
01:22:14.856 --> 01:22:17.028
Prayer was the thing.

1015
01:22:17.028 --> 01:22:21.371
<v ARSENEAULT>Did most people in Mossville believe in healing?

1016
01:22:21.371 --> 01:22:23.000
With prayer?

1017
01:22:23.000 --> 01:22:27.706
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I don't know about the healing with prayer.

1018
01:22:27.706 --> 01:22:36.596
I know that the people was believers in God, and faithful believers that, you know, didn't waver

1019
01:22:36.596 --> 01:22:46.532
<c.p>from their faith, because majority of us went to church every Sunday, and you didn't do work on Sundays.

1020
01:22:46.532 --> 01:22:54.376
More or less follow what the Bible said as to how you supposed to live.

1021
01:22:54.376 --> 01:23:03.789
But some of the churches did not practice that, the healing, the laying on hands and the oil.

1022
01:23:03.789 --> 01:23:07.972
A lot of churches didn't believe in that.

1023
01:23:07.972 --> 01:23:16.862
So I'm not quite sure about, you know, whether the Baptists and the Methodists did or not.

1024
01:23:16.862 --> 01:23:29.413
But I know there were times . . . I guess it was such a small group of people that they were all like on one Sunday,

1025
01:23:29.413 --> 01:23:32.550
everybody would go to Mount Zion.

1026
01:23:32.550 --> 01:23:43.009
The next Sunday they would go to Saint Paul, and then the next Sunday they would go to Morning Star.

1027
01:23:43.009 --> 01:23:56.606
So they all worshiped together at times, and then the one Sunday they would have their own communion and everything, you know at their own church.

1028
01:23:56.606 --> 01:23:57.651
Different churches.

1029
01:23:57.651 --> 01:24:03.404
So they communed together, and you know, worshipped together, for years.

1030
01:24:03.404 --> 01:24:10.202
And then I guess after they membership started building up, then they stopped.

1031
01:24:10.202 --> 01:24:15.431
They started just, you know, being at their own churches.

1032
01:24:15.431 --> 01:24:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's interesting.

1033
01:24:17.000 --> 01:24:17.360
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs]

1034
01:24:17.360 --> 01:24:19.160
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a community of faith.

1035
01:24:19.160 --> 01:24:19.520
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Pardon]?

1036
01:24:19.520 --> 01:24:21.680
<v ARSENEAULT>It's like a community of faith.

1037
01:24:21.680 --> 01:24:22.040
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Right.

1038
01:24:22.040 --> 01:24:22.400
Yeah.

1039
01:24:22.400 --> 01:24:25.640
<v ARSENEAULT>Aright, well, I think we could talk all day.

1040
01:24:25.640 --> 01:24:26.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs]

1041
01:24:26.000 --> 01:24:28.520
<v ARSENEAULT>And we'd probably both be really tired.

1042
01:24:28.520 --> 01:24:32.480
So just thank you so much for sharing with us and . . .

1043
01:24:32.480 --> 01:24:33.560
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>You're welcome, yeah.

1044
01:24:33.560 --> 01:24:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . talking with us.

1045
01:24:35.000 --> 01:24:35.857
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I enjoyed this.

1046
01:24:35.857 --> 01:24:39.571
I did think, I said, "What can I say for an hour?" [Laughs]

1047
01:24:39.571 --> 01:24:40.714
<v ARSENEAULT>I told you! [Laughs]

1048
01:24:40.714 --> 01:24:41.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs]

1049
01:24:41.000 --> 01:24:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>We'll have to set up a follow-up interview, and we have some other . . .

1050
01:24:45.000 --> 01:24:45.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4486.

1051
01:24:45.000 --> 01:24:45.200
End Session I.]
